# netsurf: querySelectorAll's JS path tries a candidate only against its own seed's selectors (patch 402)
# Generated 2026-11-07
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 396)
#
# github.com spent 5.7 s (host sim; far past the Vita's 8000 ms script
# deadline) in ONE MutationObserver delivery. Its selector-observer's
# SelectorSet.queryAll joins every registered selector into one
# querySelectorAll ("a, b, [attr], ..."); a few use attributes or
# pseudo-classes, so the whole list fails plain() and goes to jsQuery,
# which took one native candidate query per selector's seed (the subject's
# tag/#id/.classes; '*' for attribute-only ones) and tried EVERY candidate
# against the WHOLE list: seeds x candidates x selectors. Measured: 2.4
# million String.replace calls, 99% compoundOk's class normalisation.
#
#  - seedGroups(list): selectors grouped by identical seed. A seed is a
#    necessary condition of its selector's subject, so every element a
#    selector matches is among its seed's candidates: a candidate is tried
#    only against its group (complexOk per selector), each distinct seed
#    queried once. Same results; pseudo-element subjects still never match.
#  - compoundOk: within one jsQuery (ctx.cls), a node's class string is
#    normalised once, not per selector tried on it. matches()/closest()
#    pass no cache and behave as before.
#
# github: slowest MO delivery 5667 -> 42 ms, all MO callbacks 6.7 s ->
# 0.15 s. Results checked on github itself: 348 long-list queries compared
# against a brute-force el.matches(list) walk, 0 differ (both binaries).
# Gate: JS-selectors gains four multi-seed lists (Chromium's output), 89.
#
